To focus on an element found by `getElementsByTagName` in JavaScript, you can use the `focus()` method on the element. However, since `getElementsByTagName` returns a collection of elements, you need to specify which element in the collection you want to focus on. Here are a few ways to do this:

1. Get the first element:
```
var elements = document.getElementsByTagName("input");
elements[0].focus();
```
This will focus on the first `input` element found in the document.

2. Get the nth element:
```
var elements = document.getElementsByTagName("input");
elements[2].focus(); // Focus on the 3rd input element (0-indexed)
```
Make sure to replace the number with the correct index of the element you want to focus on.

3. Get the element by name or id:
```
var element = document.getElementsByTagName("input")[0];
if (element && element.name === "myInput") {
  element.focus();
}
```
This will focus on the first `input` element with the name "myInput".

4. Use a loop to iterate over the elements and focus on the first one that matches a condition:
```
var elements = document.getElementsByTagName("input");
for (var i = 0; i < elements.length; i++) {
  if (elements[i].getAttribute("data-my Condition") === "true") {
    elements[i].focus();
    break;
  }
}
```
This will focus on the first `input` element that has a `data-my-Condition` attribute set
